"Plots model residuals against fitted values to expose systematic shape, changing spread, and unusual observations.\n\nUse when: A fitted model needs an assessment of mean-form and variance assumptions. Original observations can be matched to their fitted values.\n\nAvoid when: The model requires a specialized residual definition that has not been chosen or explained.\n\nVisual grammar: Horizontal position encodes fitted response values. Vertical position encodes observed minus fitted response, or a named standardized residual.\n\nBuild: Compute fitted values and the appropriate residual for each included observation. Draw points against fitted values with a clear horizontal zero rule. Add an optional diagnostic smoother and label only observations needing investigation.\n\nCheck: Check the residual subtraction sign and units are stated. Verify plotted records match the fitted model’s included sample. Inspect whether transformations or weights require adjusted residuals.\n\nPitfalls: A random-looking cloud supports only the checked aspects of the model. Do not delete unusual residuals automatically; investigate their data quality and domain meaning.\n\nExample: Assess a synthetic cost model by plotting cost residuals against fitted cost, watching for curved patterns and widening spread.\n\nAlternatives: Scale-location plot, Residual sequence plot, Quantile-quantile plot."
